\section{Thermal Protection System (TPS) Engineering}
\label{sec:tps-engineering}

\subsection{Overview}
The UAP-01 atmospheric probe employs a PICA-15 ablative thermal
protection system designed to withstand extreme heating during Uranus
entry. Peak heating rates of $25{-}35~\mathrm{MW/m^2}$, shock-layer
temperatures near $10{,}000~\mathrm{K}$, and chemically reactive
nonequilibrium flows necessitate a TPS architecture capable of
simultaneous ablation, insulation, structural stability, and radiative
attenuation. This chapter derives the governing TPS equations, ablation
rates, material response, and structural constraints.

\subsection{TPS Material Properties}
PICA-15 is a low-density, carbon-based ablative material with:
\[
\rho_{\text{PICA}} \approx 0.28~\mathrm{g/cm^3},
\qquad
k_{\text{PICA}} \approx 0.03~\mathrm{W/mK},
\qquad
H_{\text{vap}} \approx 18{-}22~\mathrm{MJ/kg}.
\]

Effective emissivity:
\[
\epsilon_{\text{eff}} \approx 0.85{-}0.92.
\]

These properties enable high ablation efficiency and low thermal
conductivity.

\subsection{Heating Environment}
Total heating:
\[
q_{\text{total}} = q_{\text{conv}} + q_{\text{rad}},
\]
with:
\[
q_{\text{conv}} \sim 8{-}12~\mathrm{MW/m^2},
\qquad
q_{\text{rad}} \sim 15{-}25~\mathrm{MW/m^2}.
\]

Shock-layer chemistry (Appendix~\ref{appendix:shock-layer-chemistry})
reduces effective heating via chemical energy absorption:
\[
q_{\text{chem}} \approx 3{-}5~\mathrm{MW/m^2}.
\]

\subsection{Ablation Rate Derivation}
Mass ablation rate:
\[
\dot{m}_{\text{abl}} = \frac{q_{\text{total}} - q_{\text{chem}}}{H_{\text{vap}}}.
\]

For Uranus entry:
\[
\dot{m}_{\text{abl}}
\approx \frac{25{-}35 - 3{-}5}{18{-}22}
\approx 1.0{-}1.6~\mathrm{kg/m^2s}.
\]

Recession rate:
\[
\dot{r} = \frac{\dot{m}_{\text{abl}}}{\rho_{\text{PICA}}},
\]
yielding:
\[
\dot{r} \approx 0.3{-}0.5~\mathrm{mm/s}.
\]

\subsection{TPS Thickness Requirements}
Required TPS thickness:
\[
t_{\text{TPS}} = \int_0^{t_{\text{entry}}} \dot{r}(t)\, dt
+ t_{\text{margin}},
\]
where $t_{\text{margin}}$ accounts for:
\begin{itemize}
    \item manufacturing tolerances,
    \item off-nominal heating,
    \item shock-layer chemistry variability,
    \item structural erosion.
\end{itemize}

UUAS entry timeline:
\[
t_{\text{entry}} \approx 180{-}240~\mathrm{s}.
\]

Thus:
\[
t_{\text{TPS}} \approx (0.3{-}0.5) \times 200~\mathrm{mm}
+ t_{\text{margin}}
\approx 60{-}100~\mathrm{mm}.
\]

Final TPS thickness:
\[
t_{\text{TPS,final}} \approx 110~\mathrm{mm}.
\]

\subsection{Thermal Penetration Depth}
Thermal penetration depth:
\[
\delta = 2\sqrt{\alpha t},
\]
where:
\[
\alpha = \frac{k_{\text{PICA}}}{\rho_{\text{PICA}} c_p}.
\]

With:
\[
c_p \approx 1200~\mathrm{J/kgK},
\]
we obtain:
\[
\alpha \approx 8.9 \times 10^{-5}~\mathrm{m^2/s},
\]
yielding:
\[
\delta \approx 2\sqrt{8.9 \times 10^{-5} \times 200}
\approx 0.27~\mathrm{m}.
\]

Since ablation removes the outer layer, internal temperatures remain
below:
\[
T_{\text{internal}} < 450~\mathrm{K}.
\]

\subsection{Radiative Attenuation}
Radiative flux attenuation:
\[
q_{\text{rad,eff}} = q_{\text{rad}} (1 - \epsilon_{\text{eff}}),
\]
yielding:
\[
q_{\text{rad,eff}} \approx 2{-}4~\mathrm{MW/m^2}.
\]

This significantly reduces internal heating load.

\subsection{Structural Constraints}
TPS must withstand:
\begin{itemize}
    \item peak deceleration $a_D \approx 120{-}150~g$,
    \item pressure loads $P_{\text{peak}} \approx 18{-}22~\mathrm{kPa}$,
    \item shear forces from shock-layer detachment,
    \item vibrational loads during parachute deployment.
\end{itemize}

Structural stress:
\[
\sigma = \frac{P_{\text{peak}} R_n}{t_{\text{TPS}}},
\]
with:
\[
R_n \approx 0.65~\mathrm{m},
\]
yielding:
\[
\sigma \approx 1.2{-}1.5~\mathrm{MPa},
\]
well below PICA-15 compressive strength:
\[
\sigma_{\text{comp}} \approx 8{-}12~\mathrm{MPa}.
\]

\subsection{Bondline Temperature}
Bondline temperature:
\[
T_{\text{bond}} = T_{\text{surface}} e^{-\beta t_{\text{TPS}}},
\]
with:
\[
\beta = \sqrt{\frac{h}{k_{\text{PICA}}}},
\]
where $h$ is convective heat transfer coefficient.

UUAS predicts:
\[
T_{\text{bond}} \approx 350{-}420~\mathrm{K},
\]
below structural limits for:
\begin{itemize}
    \item avionics housing,
    \item sensor suite,
    \item parachute mortar,
    \item pressure vessel.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{TPS–Shock-Layer Coupling}
TPS performance is directly influenced by shock-layer chemistry:
\begin{itemize}
    \item H$_2$ dissociation reduces convective heating.
    \item H$_3^+$ radiative emission modifies spectral flux.
    \item CH$_x$ pyrolysis reduces stagnation temperature.
    \item carbon cluster formation increases emissivity.
\end{itemize}

UUAS THERMO-Q2 and ION-Q4 provide real-time heating corrections.

\subsection{Conclusion}
The PICA-15 TPS architecture provides sufficient ablation capacity,
thermal insulation, structural stability, and radiative attenuation to
ensure safe Uranus entry for the UAP-01 probe. Derived ablation rates,
thermal penetration depths, and bondline temperatures confirm full
compatibility with the extreme aerothermodynamic environment predicted
by UUAS multi-scale models.